86412194 13194139534146 17592186045417 true] [17592186129206 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412197 13194139534146 17592186045417 true] [17592186129206 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412200 13194139534146 17592186045417 true] [17592186129206 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412207 13194139534146 17592186045417 true] [17592186129206 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412209 13194139534146 17592186045417 true] [17592186129207 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412193 13194139534146 17592186045417 true] [17592186129207 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412199 13194139534146 17592186045417 true] [17592186129207 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412204 13194139534146 17592186045417 true] [17592186129386 1050 1380.0 13194139534146 17592186045417 true] [17592186129703 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127699 13194139534146 17592186045417 false] [17592186129703 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412183 13194139534146 17592186045417 true] [17592186129703 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534146 17592186045417 true] [17592186129703 1071 "Find the volume of the solid region enclosed by the $xy$-plane, the surface $z = y\\cos x$, and the planes $x = 0$, $x = \\frac{π}{2}$, $y = 0$, $y = 1$." 13194139534146 17592186045417 false] [17592186129703 1071 "Find the volume of the solid region enclosed by the $xy$-plane, the surface $z=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ and the planes $x=0,$ $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ $y=0,$ $y=1.$" 13194139534146 17592186045417 true] [17592186129703 1074 "Notice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[z]_{z=0}^{z=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-0\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534146 17592186045417 true] [17592186129766 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128077 13194139534146 17592186045417 false] [17592186129766 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412178 13194139534146 17592186045417 true] [17592186129766 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534146 17592186045417 true] [17592186129766 1071 "Find the volume of the solid region enclosed by the $yz$-plane, the surface $x = 6yz + 12$, and the planes $y = 0$, $y = 2$, $z = 0$, $z = 1$." 13194139534146 17592186045417 false] [17592186129766 1071 "Find the volume of the solid region enclosed by the $yz$-plane, the surface $x=6yz+12,$ and the planes $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=1.$" 13194139534146 17592186045417 true] [17592186129766 1074 "Notice that our solid can be written as a type II region: \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 6yz+12}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{6yz+12}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[x]_{x=0}^{x=6yz+12}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6yz+12)-0\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}6yz+12\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[3y{z}^{2}+12z]_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3y(1)^{2}+12(1))-0\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3y+12\\,dy \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+12y]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}({2}^{2}-0)+12(2-0) \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534146 17592186045417 true] [17592186130410 1064 "Recall that the double Integral of the function $f(x,y) = 1$ over a domain $D\\subset \\mathbb R^2$ is simply the area of $D\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}\\ \\!(D) = \\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} 1 \\cdot \\textrm d A = \\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}  \\ \\textrm d A\n$$\n\nWe have a similar result for triple integrals:\n\n*If $R$ is a region in three-dimensional space, then the volume of $R$ is given by*\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Volume}(R) = \\iiint\\limits_{R} \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nIn other words, the volume of $R$ equals the triple integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=1$ over $R.$ \n\nFor example, let's use triple integrals to find the volume of the rectangular prism shown below.\n\n![](images/8f/8f9ab5db03f1cdf84ef484aca1ab89995237e7ef333c9b7c563d0080d317e981.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written in set-builder notation as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 3 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}dx\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dy\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}dz \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We get the same value using the formula for the volume of a rectangular prism:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\text{length}\\cdot \\text{width}\\cdot \\text{height} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples." 13194139534146 17592186045417 true] [17592186130410 1064 "Recall that the double Integral of the function $f(x,y) = 1$ over a domain $D\\subset \\mathbb R^2$ is simply the area of $D\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}\\ \\!(D) = \\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} 1 \\cdot \\textrm d A = \\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}  \\ \\textrm d A\n$$\n\nWe have a similar result for triple integrals:\n\n> *If $R$ is a region in three-dimensional space, then the volume of $R$ is given by*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\textrm{Volume}(R) = \\iiint\\limits_{R} \\textrm{d}V.\n> $$\n\nIn other words, the volume of $R$ equals the triple integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=1$ over $R.$ \n\nFor example, let's use triple integrals to find the volume of the rectangular prism shown below.\n\n![](images/8f/8f9ab5db03f1cdf84ef484aca1ab89995237e7ef333c9b7c563d0080d317e981.png)\n\nNotice that our solid can be written in set-builder notation as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 3 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}dx\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dy\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}dz \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We get the same value using the formula for the volume of a rectangular prism:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\text{length}\\cdot \\text{width}\\cdot \\text{height} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples." 13194139534146 17592186045417 false] [17592186234975 1071 "Consider the solid enclosed between the paraboloids ![](images/91/9145bfbf3c349359595396d06807191dc666c03558e5943e18d6a356d3b52e80.png) and ![](images/98/98d18317cb2e69f489be66c8a4244e90327ed05aa0de5633fb6c461d81936a89.png) whose volume can be written in terms of double integrals, as follows:\n![](images/7e/7ebb2715de5217f0bd211be342bd8b64026a641254d79e7dde54606c712f0af3.png)\n\nFind ![](images/cd/cd9dbda80a5f41e2eba8b5440f42eddc12015a003e558ea26fd512cf1827a7a3.png) ![](images/54/54827258e24002d07ac1bc4468c8a8f81fa9ee932f102790093989c200860788.png) and ![](images/1b/1b0eaf7c23571a3806b1fdbcb5badeb8127cefe1f5d71ea8d1ea426aa2e0fbff.png)" 13194139534146 17592186045417 false] [17592186234975 1071 "Consider the solid enclosed between the paraboloids ![](images/df/df720fc5b6471db8190154ad9e8c1a3094f3fe885f6150de21bb7a5ef0653be5.sv